
Frank Buchman, founder of the Oxford Group that gave AA its ideological premises has said several phrases that may sound familiar:

- "Our destiny is to obey the guidance of God"

- "When man listens, God speaks. When man obeys, God acts. When men change, nations change."

- "God spoke to the prophets of old. He may speak to you. God speaks to those who listen. God acts through those who obey"

The concept of obedience over understanding repeatedly comes up. This can seem benign enough until one recalls that the Oxford Group was trying to convert Hitler in order to install a Christian dictatorship in a global superpower that would bring about a God-fearing utopia. Peter Howard, who took over Buchman's groups when he died, was openly involved in movements sporting the label "fascist" in Britain. Here's what they've said about democracy:

- "Our old system of democracy, like our old system of economics, is gone — never to return. No intermediate stage of man-made adjustment is likely to linger with us for very long. Therefore, on whether you and I accept this new awakening of the Holy Spirit, depends the outcome of present political experiments." - Vic Kitchen

- "An increasing number of citizens in democratic states are now unwilling to acknowledge in speech and action those inner authorities on which the life of democracy depends. Each man has his own plan. It's so wonderful each to have his own plan. It's such freedom, such liberty! Everyone does as he pleases. But not in the Oxford Group. There you have true democracy. You don't do as you please, you do as God guides. You do God's plan." - Buchman

- "He is a sail always waiting to be filled by the wind, a man with a great and warm and humble heart, a democrat who wants to set men free under God's dictatorship." - Herbert Grevenius

- "Man is born to believe and obey. If he turns his back on God, he turns his face to man and man's dominion. He will believe and obey Hitler or Stalin as long as they hold the whip. Then he will serve their successors." - Peter Howard

This is one the reasons Ronald Niebuhr, who wrote the original Serenity Prayer, despised Buchman and the Oxford Group on both theological and political grounds. Niebuhr was a member of the Socialist Party of the United States and was staunchly pro-union after observing the conditions of the working class in Detroit. He pointed out that we've already seen sincere Christian dictatorships, and they have not been immune to the temptations of sin or the moral complexities and ambiguities of state leadership. Buchman was thus not just claiming that he could convert evil dictators into Christian dictators, but also promising to do so in a way that was more authentic than previous Christian leaders. There is nothing I plan to say that is harsher than Niebuhr's own attitude towards this:

*The Oxford group movement, imagining itself the mediator of Christ's salvation in a catastrophic age, is really an additional evidence of the decay in which we stand. Its religion manages to combine bourgeois complacency with Christian contrition in a manner which makes the former dominant. Its morality is a religious expression of a decadent individualism. Far from offering us a way out of our difficulties it adds to the general confusion. This is not the gospel's message of judgement and hope to the world. It is bourgeois optimism, individualism and moralism expressing itself in the guise of religion. No wonder the rather jittery plutocrats of our day open their spacious summer homes to its message!*

*The increasingly obvious fascist philosophy which informs the group movement is in other words not only socially vicious but religiously vapid. The slightest acquaintance with the history of Christian thought on the problem of the relation of the absolute demands of the gospel to the relativities of politics and economics would prove its childishness. A careful study of the gospel itself, particularly its abhorrence of the self-righteousness of the righteous, would reveal the danger of any doctrine which promises powerful men the possibility of fully doing the will of God. They had better be admonished that after they have done what they think right they will still remain unprofitable servants.*

So even at the time, very notable and influential Christian theologians were alarmed by the calls for obedience in the name of the will of God, not least because any human claiming to act righteously by the will of God could be capable of leading people into great atrocities.

Let me be clear: I am in no way and by no means calling AA fascist or even proto-fascist. That's offensively reductive to a movement with so many well-intentioned people sharing resources and support for one of the most stigmatized medical conditions out there. Besides, those arguments have already been made by people with an actual axe to grind.

My point is that the mechanism of obeying before understanding has been very useful for those who launder authoritarian ideas in democratic language by promising that you will be able to freely explore why it works eventually. You actually hear the same arguments from researchers and clinicians that that we seldom understand any innovation fully before we can implement it and reap the benefits. After all, the mechanisms for CBT have never been fully explained, but the evidence that it can succeed at short-term symptom reduction seems reasonably firm. The problem is that by the time something has been shown to work and adopted widely, we necessarily also have created lasting infrastructure for distributing that solution, and the people involved in that have developed a vested interest in promoting the solution. Fully understanding it could reveal something unpleasant, like going into the kitchen of your favorite restaurant to see how many cockroaches are included in your meal, free of charge. Distributing it as is keeps a good thing going.

Niebuhr felt compelled to call this out when Buchman was scaling the distribution for the Oxford Group from an anti-institutional spiritual project to converting authoritarians of the most brutal institutions ever made. He mentions explicitly that if this were just a program for destitute alcoholics or wayward souls, he might even find something to praise in it. The ambition to construct a utopia out of a social philosophy that was very welcoming to fascist ideas is where we might imagine people stopped asking "why" and focused on "how." *Why* do we 

The idea of achieving freedom through obedience of codified law is what made Hitler and Henry Ford compelling converts for Buchman, not necessarily empowering people into navigating their own lives by a greater awareness of themselves and the power to do their own inquiries to form their own understandings.